  #block(breakable: false)[
  === Complete Solutions

  #sol-item(1, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Scarcity is a relationship between limited resources and unlimited wants, and it applies to every society, rich or poor. Poverty and a temporary shortage are narrower ideas.
  ]

  #sol-item(2, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    As output of one good grows, ever less suitable resources are transferred, so each further transfer costs more. Constant opportunity cost would give a straight line.
  ]
  ]

  #sol-item(3, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A testable claim about what happens. The other three are value judgements — they recommend, and no figure can prove them right or wrong.
  ]

  #sol-item(4, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Capital means produced means of production: machines, tools and buildings. Money in a bank is a claim on capital, not capital itself, and skill is human capital, which belongs to labour.
  ]

  #sol-item(5, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The substitute becomes dearer, so buyers switch towards tea and its demand curve shifts right. A complement becoming dearer would reduce demand.
  ]

  #sol-item(6, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Both shifts push quantity up, so quantity must rise. They push price in opposite directions, so the price change depends on which shift is larger.
  ]

  #sol-item(7, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A binding floor holds the price above equilibrium, so sellers offer more than buyers will take. The unsold surplus is why support-buying schemes exist.
  ]

  #sol-item(8, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Suppliers, not buyers, determine supply: the number of sellers, input prices and technology. The other three act on the demand side.
  ]

  #sol-item(9, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A surplus means unsold stock, and unsold stock is expensive to hold, so sellers undercut one another until the price reaches equilibrium.
  ]

  #sol-item(10, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A tax raises the cost of production, so less is offered at every price. A rise in the good's own price is a movement along the curve, not a shift.
  ]

  #sol-item(11, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Set 100 − 3P = 20 + 2P, so 80 = 5P and P = ₦16; then Qs = 20 + 32 = 52. Each other pair fails on substitution.
  ]

  #sol-item(12, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The good's own price moves the seller along the supply curve. The other three are determinants of supply, and each shifts the whole curve.
  ]

  #sol-item(13, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Quantity falls 10 per cent and price rises 10 per cent, so PED = 10 ÷ 10 = 1.0, unit elastic. Reading the absolute change rather than the percentage gives 20 ÷ 5 = 4.
  ]

  #sol-item(14, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A small budget share and few substitutes both make buyers unresponsive. Many substitutes would make demand elastic, and a luxury good usually has an elasticity above one.
  ]

  #sol-item(15, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    PED = 5 ÷ 15 = 0.33, which is below one, so demand is inelastic. Inverting the division gives 3.0, the classic slip.
  ]

  #sol-item(16, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A price rise for one reduces demand for the other, so the changes move in opposite directions and the elasticity is negative. Substitutes give a positive figure.
  ]

  #sol-item(17, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Marginal product turns down at the point of diminishing returns, while total product is still rising. Total product falls only later, when marginal product becomes negative.
  ]

  #sol-item(18, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    TFC never changes, so dividing it by a growing Q must give a falling AFC. The curve is a rectangular hyperbola and never touches either axis.
  ]

  #sol-item(19, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    AFC = TFC ÷ Q = 600 ÷ 30 = ₦20. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 18,000, which is a common slip under time pressure.
  ]

  #sol-item(20, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Normal profit is the cost of the entrepreneur's own effort and capital; it is earned when AR = AC, and it is included in cost. Supernormal profit is the excess above it.
  ]

  #sol-item(21, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Free entry and exit compete any supernormal profit away, so price settles at minimum average cost. That is the long-run equilibrium the examiner tests.
  ]

  #sol-item(22, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The seller must charge more where demand is inelastic, so the markets must be separable and resale must be impossible. A single market cannot be charged two prices.
  ]

  #sol-item(23, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A cartel is an agreement among a few large firms to act like a monopoly — which presupposes that there are a few. A single seller needs no agreement.
  ]

  #sol-item(24, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The income approach adds wages, rent, interest and profit. The output approach adds value added, and the expenditure approach adds consumption, investment, government spending and net exports.
  ]

  #sol-item(25, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Households own the factors, so they sell factor services and receive income, which they spend on the goods firms produce. Saving is a leakage from that flow, not a reason to stand outside it.
  ]

  #sol-item(26, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Multiplier = 1 ÷ (1 − MPC) = 1 ÷ 0.2 = 5. Dividing by the MPC itself gives 1.25, which is the reciprocal of the wrong number.
  ]

  #sol-item(27, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Spending above revenue in a year, financed by borrowing. The export–import gap is the trade balance, which is a different account.
  ]

  #sol-item(28, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Goods, services, income and financial flows, all in one account. Restricting it to goods records only the visible trade balance.
  ]

  #sol-item(29, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A weaker currency lowers the foreign price of exports and raises the domestic price of imports, which should improve the trade balance if demand responds.
  ]

  #sol-item(30, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A tariff is a tax on imports, and it raises their domestic price. A tax on goods sold inside the country is an excise or sales tax, not a tariff.
  ]

  #sol-item(31, "B", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Income, schooling and health — three dimensions of welfare. The index exists because income per head alone says nothing about how long or how well people live.
  ]

  #sol-item(32, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A young population has many dependants per worker, so consumption pressure rises before the children become producers. Income per head can fall even while total output grows.
  ]

  #sol-item(33, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The standard definition: able, willing and without work. Working fewer hours is underemployment, and having stopped looking removes a person from the labour force.
  ]

  #sol-item(34, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    It is the rate at which the central bank lends to the banks, and it anchors every other interest rate in the economy. Company tax is fiscal, and the exchange rate is a separate instrument.
  ]

  #sol-item(35, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    A larger share of deposits must be held with the central bank, so less can be lent, and the credit multiplier falls. The effect on the exchange rate is indirect and uncertain.
  ]

  #sol-item(36, "C", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Direct taxes are levied on income and wealth, and their burden cannot be passed on. VAT, import duty and excise are indirect taxes, collected by firms and passed to the buyer in the price.
  ]

  #sol-item(37, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Interest and principal are paid out of revenue, which is why a large debt service bill crowds out spending on services. Reserves may be used to repay external debt, but they are not the routine source.
  ]

  #sol-item(38, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    Growth is a bigger total; development is a better life — income, health, schooling, and a changing structure of production. Growth without development is the case the examiner describes as jobless growth.
  ]

  #sol-item(39, "D", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    The National Bureau of Statistics compiles GDP, inflation and unemployment figures. The central bank publishes monetary and balance-of-payments data.
  ]

  #sol-item(40, "A", dense: true, pad: 22.27pt)[
    OPEC, as a major oil producer and exporter. The OECD is a club of mostly high-income economies, and the EU and NATO are not African organisations.
